Step 10 of Theorem 4.2.1 (p. 123) says '(S̄, W̄) → (S, W) is a Kummer étale morphism of log regular log schemes, and hence the composition (X̄, T̄) → (S̄, W̄) → (S, W) is log regular'. This uses three results:
- the log form of Abhyankar's lemma;
- Kato's theorem that a log smooth scheme over a log regular one is log regular;
- the log smoothness of the semistable multipointed curve.
No item states any of them, and no stage plans them. The Abhyankar arguments in the atlas are LPV.5, AlgebraicCurves layer 8 and IG.5, and CR.5 plans only the definitions and chart criteria of log smoothness. The fix is right. Kato, Toric singularities (1994), is already a prerequisite, so only Grothendieck–Murre (LNM 208) or SGA 1 Exposé XIII §5 needs adding. Medium is right.

The four uses are as stated: constructible compactness (proof of Lemma 3.1.3, p. 113), openness and constructibility of images (proof of Lemma 3.1.6, p. 115), and valuation rings dominating local rings (p. 115). No item states them. The Mathlib 082e2d3 declarations exist:
- LocalSubring.exists_le_valuationSubring (RingTheory/Valuation/LocalSubring.lean:118);
- Flat.generalizingMap and isOpenMap_of_generalizingMap (AlgebraicGeometry/Morphisms/UniversallyOpen.lean:131, 107);
- Scheme.Hom.isConstructible_image (AlgebraicGeometry/Morphisms/FinitePresentation.lean:161);
- compactSpace_withConstructibleTopology, which applies to schemes since Mathlib makes every scheme quasi-sober and prespectral.
The library statuses in the fix are justified. Low is right.
